=== task: multi medium
title: Ways to count copies
prompt: `items` is sorted. Choose **every** expression that gives the number of copies of `x`, including 0 when x isn't there.
* `bisect_right(items, x) - bisect_left(items, x)` :: Right. The copies sit between the two places; with no copies, the places are equal.
* `items.count(x)` :: Right, but it checks every item: O(n) instead of O(log n).
* `len([v for v in items if v == x])` :: Right, but it also looks at every item: O(n).
- `bisect_right(items, x) - bisect_left(items, x) + 1` :: The right place is already one past the last copy, so this counts one too many.
- `last_index(items, x) - first_index(items, x) + 1`, with the functions from this lesson :: When x is missing, both are −1, and this gives −1 − (−1) + 1 = 1. It needs a check for −1 first.
- `bisect_left(items, x)` :: That's the number of items smaller than x.
hint: Try each one on a list that doesn't contain x.
hint: The right place is one past the last copy.
right: Two insertion points give the count in O(log n). `.count` and the comprehension are also right, but look at every item.
wrong: `bisect_right - bisect_left` is the count, with no + 1. `.count` and the comprehension are correct but O(n). The first/last version says a missing value appears once unless you check for −1.

